/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A closed system stores energy in three forms.
   - E is total energy in J; U is internal energy in J; m is mass in kg; c is the speed of the system as a whole in m/s (this unit writes speed as c so that V always means volume); g = 9.81 m/s²; z is height in m above a chosen datum.
   - U has no measurable zero.
   - In a rigid closed tank with no shaft, ΔKE and ΔPE are zero, so ΔE = ΔU.
   - Work crosses a boundary when a force moves its point of application: W = ∫F dx, force in newtons, distance in metres, work in joules.
   - Sign rule: work done by the system on the surroundings is positive.
   - 1 J = 1 N·m; 1 kJ = 1000 J.
   - Heat is energy crossing a boundary because of a temperature difference.
   - The dot means rate in W.
   - For a plain temperature change of a liquid or solid, Q = m cp ΔT, with cp the specific heat in J/(kg·K) (lower-case c for per kilogram; capital C for the heat capacity of a whole body in J/K) (see 1.7).
*/
