/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Two bodies are in thermal contact when energy can cross the wall between them as heat.
   - An empirical temperature is a number assigned by a chosen thermometer.
   - θ is the empirical temperature; X is the thermometric property, such as mercury length or platinum resistance; a and b are constants fixed by two fixed points.
   - Older scales use the ice point (0 °C, 32 °F) and steam point (100 °C, 212 °F) at 1 atm.
   - T(K) is absolute temperature in kelvin; t(°C) is Celsius temperature.
   - t(°F) is Fahrenheit temperature; T(R) is Rankine temperature.
   - Temperature is intensive; doubling the mass does not double the reading.
   - Liquid-in-glass and resistance thermometers each give a slightly different number between their fixed points, because mercury, alcohol and platinum do not change in exactly the same way.
   - T is the ideal-gas temperature in K; P is the gas pressure at the unknown temperature; Ptp is the gas pressure at the triple point of water, both at the same fixed volume.
   - Different gases (hydrogen, nitrogen, helium) give slightly different readings when the bulb is well filled.
*/
