/* book Worked example 2: Q: A constant-volume hydrogen thermometer reads Ptp = 40.0 kPa at the triple point of water and 54.6 kPa in boiling liquid. Find the temperature. Separately, a platinum resistance thermometer reads 100.0 Ω at 0 °C and 138.5 Ω at 100 °C; on a linear scale θ = aR + b, what Celsius value does a reading of 119.4 Ω give?
   1. Gas thermometer: T = 273.16 × P/Ptp = 273.16 × 54.6/40.0 = 273.16 × 1.365 = 372.86 K, that is 372.86 − 273.15 = 99.71 °C. (A true ideal-gas reading needs Ptp taken toward zero; at 40 kPa the value is close but not the limit.)
   2. Resistance thermometer, two fixed points: a = (100 − 0)/(138.5 − 100.0) = 2.597 °C per Ω and b = −100.0a = −259.7 °C.
   3. θ = 2.597 × 119.4 − 259.7 = 50.39 °C. Equivalently θ = 100 × (119.4 − 100.0)/(138.5 − 100.0) = 100 × 19.4/38.5 = 50.39 °C.
   Answer: The gas thermometer gives 372.86 K (99.71 °C); the platinum thermometer on its linear scale reads 50.39 °C. */
